---
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import PageShell from '../../components/nav/PageShell.astro';
import { rustDark, rustLight } from '../../../shiki-rust-themes.mjs';
import { CRATE_URL, DOCSRS_URL } from '../../consts';

const SHIKI = { light: rustLight, dark: rustDark };

const title = 'How QCL works | DeepCausality Quantum';
const description =
  'A QCL program configures a subject, validates it with checks that report what they examined, and controls it with experiments the planner chooses. Failures name what broke.';

const shape = `config(subject, candidates)  →  validate(checks)  →  Screened  →  control(evidence)  →  report`;

const vacuous = `check_markov         Accepted  examined 1  worst margin 0.000e0
check_decomposable   Vacuous   examined 0  worst margin 0.000e0`;

const failure = `[2] Non-commuting model: σx and σz on leg 0
    ✓ validate failed: factors at nodes 0 and 1 do not commute
    the subject is as built: is_frozen() = true

[3] The same non-commuting model on a dynamic graph, through the shipped freeze
    ✓ freeze aborted: factors at nodes 0 and 1 do not commute
    is_frozen() = false (rolled back)`;

const law = `[Z̄(0)] at the shipped precisions
         f32: e1 = 1.19e-6, e2 = 4.77e-7, |t1|_pre = 1.000000, |t2|_post = 2.000000, bound = 2.86e-6, measured = 9.54e-7, holds
         f64: e1 = 2.22e-15, e2 = 1.78e-15, |t1|_pre = 1.000000, |t2|_post = 2.000000, bound = 6.22e-15, measured = 1.78e-15, holds
    Float106: e1 = 0.00e0, e2 = 0.00e0, |t1|_pre = 1.000000, |t2|_post = 2.000000, bound = 0.00e0, measured = 0.00e0, holds`;
---
<PageShell title={title} description={description} path="/how-it-works/">
  <header class="doc-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">How it works</p>
    <h1>A QCL program configures, validates and controls.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      QCL, the Quantum Causal Language, is a builder in Rust. You name a subject and its
      candidates; the builder checks them, and the candidates that pass go to a stage that plans
      experiments and reads their results. Every check reports what it examined, and every failure
      names what broke.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="shape">
    <h2>What is the shape of a program?</h2>
    <p>A program calls <code>config</code>, then <code>validate</code>, then <code>control</code>, and the compiler enforces that order.</p>
    <Code code={shape} lang="text" themes={SHIKI} wrap />
    <p>
      <code>QclBuilder::config</code> names two working types once: a real type for accuracy and a
      count type for headroom. The count type moves no threshold. <code>build()</code> refuses a
      subject that would answer unsoundly, before any stage runs; an unfrozen graph, a cyclic
      causal structure and a plant with no candidates are refused there.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>validate</code> ends in a <code>Screened</code> value: the candidates that passed every
      check, with the report. A structural candidate reaches <code>control</code> only through that
      value, so no path skips the checks. A plant configured with structural candidates has no
      <code>control</code> implementation, and the compiler rejects the call.
    </p>
    <p>
      The pipeline orders and names the stages of the causal monad, the crate’s effect type. It
      defines no new monad.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="subjects">
    <h2>What can you name as the subject?</h2>
    <p>
      You name one of four subjects: a plant, a model, a code or a circuit. The subject decides
      which checks exist. Only a plant reaches <code>control</code>; the other three stop after
      <code>validate</code>.
    </p>
    <dl class="deflist">
      <div>
        <dt>Plant</dt>
        <dd>
          A system that evolves and is measured, with the observables it exposes and its
          candidates. A structural candidate claims a causal structure and goes through the screen.
          A mechanism candidate claims none and enters control directly. Checks:
          <code>check_markov</code>, <code>check_decomposable</code>. Example:
          <a href="/examples/qcl-crosstalk/">crosstalk</a>.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Model</dt>
        <dd>
          A process written as one Choi–Jamiołkowski operator per node of a frozen graph, meaning a
          graph with fixed node ids that passed the freeze checks. It behaves as a plant with one
          structural candidate and no evidence, and the existing freeze check maps onto it. Checks:
          <code>check_markov</code>, <code>check_decomposable</code>. Example:
          <a href="/examples/qcl-qcm-freeze/">freeze</a>.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Code</dt>
        <dd>
          A chain complex that defines a CSS code, evaluated exactly. It has no experiments, no
          baseline and no evidence, so it offers validate stages only: <code>derive_code</code>,
          <code>check_ldpc_weights</code>, <code>check_class_invariance</code>,
          <code>check_clifford_action</code>. Example:
          <a href="/examples/qcl-geometric-qec/">toric code</a>.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Circuit</dt>
        <dd>
          A compositional circuit model. The library stores the circuit and screens its dilation,
          the circuit rewritten as a factorization the checks can read. Only a circuit can enter an
          abstraction. Checks:
          <code>check_markov</code>, <code>check_decomposable</code>,
          <code>check_alignment_structure</code>, <code>check_naturality</code>. Example:
          <a href="/examples/qcl-crosstalk-circuits/">crosstalk over circuits</a>.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="checks">
    <h2>What does a check report?</h2>
    <p>
      Every check returns one shape: the quantity it measured, its threshold, the margin (a ratio
      of the two; 1 or below accepts) and the number of items it examined. The verdict is
      Accepted, Rejected or Vacuous. Vacuous means that nothing was examined, so nothing was
      certified.
    </p>
    <p>
      A bare pass would hide that case. On a two-node chain no 3×3 sub-relation exists, because one
      needs three inputs and three outputs. So <code>check_decomposable</code> has nothing to
      search, and the report says so.
    </p>
    <Code code={vacuous} lang="text" themes={SHIKI} wrap />
    <p>
      The tolerances form one family with five named members: commutator, validation, numerical
      rank, state and shot noise. The first four derive from the machine epsilon of the working
      real type, so moving from <code>f64</code> to <code>f32</code> moves those thresholds with
      it. Shot noise derives from the shot count.
    </p>
    <p>
      A rejection has three outcomes, set by the stage and the subject. On a plant,
      <code>check_markov</code> and <code>check_decomposable</code> drop a failing candidate from
      the admitted set. On a model or a circuit, a rejection from those two checks fails the
      stage, and <code>finalize()</code> returns the error; on a circuit,
      <code>check_alignment_structure</code> and <code>check_naturality</code> fail the stage the
      same way. The code checks <code>check_ldpc_weights</code>,
      <code>check_class_invariance</code> and <code>check_clifford_action</code> record the
      rejection in the report, and <code>finalize()</code> returns <code>Ok</code>. A structural
      error, such as a shape mismatch, fails the stage in every case.
    </p>
    <p class="next-links">
      <a href="/checks/">Every check, and what backs it →</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="control">
    <h2>What does control do?</h2>
    <p>
      Control turns candidates into a decision: it forks one world per candidate, measures or
      predicts in each world, and adjudicates the verdicts. Two stage orders are valid.
      A mechanism plant runs <code>fork</code>, <code>observe</code>, <code>gate</code>,
      <code>adjudicate</code>. A structural plant runs <code>observe</code>, <code>fork</code>,
      <code>predict</code>, <code>compare</code>, <code>adjudicate</code>. The stage
      <code>design</code> belongs to neither order, because it needs only the candidates and the
      experiments on offer.
    </p>
    <dl class="deflist">
      <div><dt><code>fork</code></dt><dd>Creates one world per candidate, each with its own ledger, and fails if the screen admitted no candidate. A structural candidate’s world holds the plant unchanged; a mechanism candidate’s world holds the plant evolved by that candidate’s channel.</dd></div>
      <div><dt><code>observe</code></dt><dd>Measures an observable over a number of shots: the plant before the fork, each world’s plant after it.</dd></div>
      <div><dt><code>predict</code></dt><dd>Evaluates each world’s model for an observable, without spending device time.</dd></div>
      <div><dt><code>compare</code></dt><dd>Judges each world’s prediction against the observation, in standard errors of the observation.</dd></div>
      <div><dt><code>gate</code></dt><dd>Judges a read-out against a specification: at least a given value, or at most one.</dd></div>
      <div><dt><code>design</code></dt><dd>Solves the minimum-cost cover: the cheapest experiments that separate each coverable pair of candidates by a floor of bits. It also lists the pairs no experiment reaches.</dd></div>
      <div><dt><code>adjudicate</code></dt><dd>Folds the worlds’ verdicts and names a survivor, or names why the data leaves several candidates standing.</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <p>
      The ledger counts shots, experiments, predictions, device time, cost and bits. A shot budget
      draws down through checked subtraction, and an overdrawn budget returns an error that names
      the shortfall.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="failure">
    <h2>What happens when something fails?</h2>
    <p>
      The error names the offender, and the subject stays as it was built. A non-commuting pair
      (<code>CommutatorNonZero</code>) carries the two node indices as typed fields. A Pauli
      outside the code’s normalizer (<code>NotInNormalizer</code>) carries the index of the
      stabilizer generator it anticommutes with. The triples of a C₃
      (<code>NotFaithfullyRepresentable</code>) and the name of a cyclic candidate
      (<code>CyclicStructureUnsupported</code>) appear in the error’s message.
    </p>
    <p>
      A stage mutates nothing, so a failed <code>validate</code> leaves the subject as it was
      built. A failed freeze rolls the graph back to its dynamic state, so a model that cannot
      freeze is never left half frozen.
    </p>
    <Code code={failure} lang="text" themes={SHIKI} wrap />
    <p class="next-links">
      <a href={`${CRATE_URL}/src/error/quantum_error.rs`}>Every error variant in the source ↗</a>
      <a href={DOCSRS_URL}>API reference on docs.rs ↗</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="precision">
    <h2>How do you choose the precision?</h2>
    <p>
      You change one type alias. Each example declares <code>type FloatType = Float106;</code>,
      and the factors, the separations, the plan’s costs and every read-out recompute at that
      precision. Switch it to <code>f32</code>, <code>f64</code> or <code>BFloat16</code> and no
      other line of source changes. The results and thresholds move, and some examples state where
      a narrow type stops meeting their tolerance.
    </p>
    <p>
      A hard-coded <code>f64</code> stays invisible while the alias is <code>f64</code> and fails
      to compile the moment the two differ. For that reason the examples default to
      <code>Float106</code>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="abstractions">
    <h2>What is an abstraction, and what does QCL do with one?</h2>
    <p>
      An abstraction relates a detailed model to a coarser one, and QCL measures how far the
      relation is from exact. Eight physical qubits carry two logical qubits, and the logical model
      describes the same behaviour with fewer parts. Lorenz and Tull (arXiv:2602.16612) define when
      such a map is sound: it must commute with every query in a chosen signature, a set of
      queries.
    </p>
    <p>
      QCL measures the failure to commute as a residual, so an approximate abstraction reports its
      distance from exact. When two abstractions compose, the composition law bounds the
      composite’s residual: <code>ε ≤ ‖τ₂‖_post · ε₁ + ‖τ₁‖_pre · ε₂</code>. Here ε₁ and ε₂ are
      the two links’ residuals, and τ₁ and τ₂ are their channel maps. The crate computes both
      norms for each query. In the Frobenius norm on Choi operators neither equals 1 in general,
      because a partial trace over a factor of dimension d has norm √d.
    </p>
    <p>
      The concatenated-code example prints the law for the logical gate Z̄(0) at three precisions.
      At <code>f32</code> the bound is 2 × 1.19e-6 + 1 × 4.77e-7 = 2.86e-6, and the measured
      residual is 9.54e-7. At each precision the measured residual is at or under the bound.
    </p>
    <Code code={law} lang="text" themes={SHIKI} />
    <p>
      When both residuals are zero the composite is exact, and Lean proves that case
      (<code>quantum.abstraction.compose_exact</code>). The bound for nonzero residuals follows
      from the triangle inequality, and Lean has no statement of it.
    </p>
    <p>
      A decoder is also an abstraction, from a circuit to its detector error model. The crate
      contains no decoding algorithm; the decoder’s reading of the record enters as data. The
      library injects a fault at each named circuit location and checks the model against the
      circuit. A correlated error that the model omits produces the worst failure, and the failure
      names the location. Tests cover this path, and no example runs it.
    </p>
    <p class="next-links">
      <a href="/examples/qcl-concatenated-code/">Concatenated code example →</a>
      <a href="/examples/qcl-code-switching/">Code switching example →</a>
      <a href="/examples/qcl-distillation-round/">Distillation example →</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</PageShell>
